            "
          >
            Analysis
          </h2>
        </mj-text>
        <mj-text
          padding="10px 12px"
          font-family="TNYAdobeCaslonPro, 'Times New Roman', serif;"
          color="#000000"
        >
          <h2 style="font-size: 24px; line-height: 1.2; font-weight: 500; margin: 0;">
  War hits FIFA qualification
</h2>
<p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 10px 0 0 0;"><strong>Summary: War disruption threatens Iraq’s historic World Cup qualification chance after forty years.</strong></p>
<p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 10px 0 0 0;"><strong>What’s happening?</strong> Iraq’s men’s national football team faces uncertainty about its chances of <a href="https://edition.cnn.com/2026/03/10/sport/iran-iraq-fifa-world-cup-berth" target="_blank" style="text-decoration: none; border-bottom: 2px solid #06266d; color: black;">qualifying for the 2026 FIFA World Cup</a> amid regional conflict, which is disrupting travel plans. The team is scheduled to play the winner of a playoff between Bolivia and Suriname in Monterrey, Mexico, on March 31 for a spot in the tournament. However, the war in neighboring Iran has led to the closure of Iraqi airspace, preventing most players and staff from leaving the country.</p>
<p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 10px 0 0 0;"><strong>Demand:</strong> The Iraq Football Association (IFA) has formally asked FIFA to postpone the playoff qualifier. Around 60% of the squad remains stranded in Iraq, while some support staff are stuck in other countries due to disrupted flights and visa complications. Iraq’s coach, Graham Arnold, says delaying the game would give the team a fair possibility to travel, regroup, and properly prepare for a crucial match.</p>
<p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 10px 0 0 0;"><strong>Big picture:</strong> The war is threatening Iraq’s possibility of qualifying for the World Cup for the first time in 40 years. This crisis also places FIFA in a difficult position, balancing fairness to competing teams with extraordinary circumstances caused by war. The situation highlights how external crises can jeopardize years of sporting progress and deny teams a rare possibility to compete on football’s biggest stage.</p>
        </mj-text>
      </mj-column>
    </mj-section>
    <mj-spacer height="10px" />
    <mj-section
