            "
          >
            Foundations
          </h2>
        </mj-text>
        <mj-text
  padding="10px 12px"
  font-family="TNYAdobeCaslonPro, 'Times New Roman', serif;"
  color="#000000"
>
  <h2 style="font-size: 24px; line-height: 1.2; font-weight: 500; margin: 0;">
    What is a frozen conflict?
  </h2>
</mj-text>
<mj-image
  border="1px solid #00000013"
  border-radius="10px"
  padding="10px 12px"
  width="600px"
  src="https://www.geopoliticalsummary.com/email/images/REPLACE_ME.jpg"
  alt="REPLACE_ME"
  href="https://www.geopoliticalsummary.com/"
/>

<mj-text padding="10px 12px" font-family="Roboto+Serif" color="#000000">
  <p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 0 0 10px 0;"><strong>What is it?</strong> A frozen conflict occurs when active fighting has stopped, but no peace agreement has been reached, leaving the dispute unresolved. This happens when the deeper issues, such as sovereignty, borders, governance, and national identity, remain unsettled. While a <a href="https://www.teseopress.com/pazvisionesestrategiasluchas/chapter/frozen-conflicts-a-concept-describing-the-realities-between-2/" target="_blank" style="text-decoration: none; border-bottom: 2px solid #06266d; color: black;">frozen conflict could lead to sporadic violence</a>, a full-scale war will not occur. These conflicts create prolonged instability, hinder economic development, and compromise protections for human rights.</p>
<p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 0 0 10px 0;"><strong>Impact:</strong> Frozen conflicts often arise from ceasefires, armistices, or external interventions that halt violence without achieving a long-term political resolution. It can lead to the creation of unrecognized entities or states. These territory ends up being controlled by breakaway regions or de facto authorities that lack international recognition but maintain their own political or military structures. Foreign powers often play influential roles, either as peacekeepers or as supporters of one side, further complicating resolution.</p>
<p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 0 0 10px 0;"><strong>Examples:</strong> The frozen conflicts in Transnistria in Moldova, Abkhazia, and South Ossetia in Georgia stem from post-Soviet disputes. Russia serves as a guarantor state, as its involvement in these conflicts has prevented Georgia and Moldova from drifting toward Western institutions. The Korean Peninsula is another classic case in which a technical state of war still exists.</p>
</mj-text>
